\section{Introduction}\label{sec:instruction-selection-intro}

\emph{Instruction selection} is the phase of code generation that maps the intermediate representation (IR) of a program to a sequence of target machine instructions. Given an IR tree or DAG representing a computation, instruction selection chooses the best sequence of machine instructions to implement it, respecting the target architecture's instruction set, addressing modes, and register constraints.

The instruction selection problem is fundamentally a \emph{pattern matching} problem: each machine instruction implements a specific pattern of operations, and the goal is to cover the IR with a minimum-cost set of instruction patterns. This chapter develops the theory and practice of instruction selection, from tree parsing with tree automata to the BURS (Bottom-Up Rewrite System) algorithm and modern table-driven approaches.

Part~XXVIII distinguishes finding legal matches from choosing a compatible
set of matches. Use \cref{ch:ext-265,ch:ext-267,ch:ext-268} to compare tree
parsing, least-cost covering, and generated tables. Then compare a tree
selector against models that preserve shared values
(\cref{ch:ext-270,ch:ext-274,ch:ext-277}). The validation methods in
\cref{ch:ext-278} separate correctness from cost-model optimality.

\section{Pattern Matching on Trees and DAGs}\label{sec:pattern-matching}

\subsection{The IR as Trees and DAGs}\label{sec:ir-trees}

The intermediate representation is typically organized as a collection of \emph{expression trees} (for expressions without shared subexpressions) or \emph{expression DAGs} (for expressions with shared subexpressions).

\begin{definition}[Expression Tree]\label{def:expr-tree}
An \textbf{expression tree} is a tree where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Leaf nodes represent constants or memory loads.
  \item Internal nodes represent operations (add, multiply, etc.).
  \item Each node is labeled with an operator from a ranked alphabet.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Expression Tree]\label{ex:expr-tree}
The expression \texttt{a[i] = x * 4 + 1} is represented as:
\begin{verbatim}
        STORE
       /     \
    ADDR     ADD
   /    \   /   \
  a     MUL  CONST(1)
       /   \
     ADD   CONST(4)
    /   \
   x     i
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Machine Description as Patterns}\label{sec:machine-patterns}

Each target machine instruction is described as a \emph{tree pattern} with an associated \emph{cost}.

\begin{definition}[Machine Instruction Pattern]\label{def:machine-pattern}
A \textbf{machine instruction pattern} is a tree pattern with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A \emph{root label} matching the IR operator.
  \item \emph{Child patterns} matching the operands.
  \item A \emph{cost} (typically the instruction latency or code size).
  \item A \emph{side effect} (the assembly instruction emitted).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[x86 Instruction Patterns]\label{ex:x86-patterns}
\begin{verbatim}
  reg -> CONST(c)         { cost 1; emit "mov reg, c" }
  reg -> ADD(reg, reg)    { cost 1; emit "add reg, reg" }
  reg -> ADD(reg, CONST)  { cost 2; emit "add reg, imm" }
  reg -> MUL(reg, reg)    { cost 3; emit "imul reg, reg" }
  reg -> LOAD(ADDR)       { cost 2; emit "mov reg, [addr]" }
  reg -> MEM(reg)         { cost 1; emit "mov reg, [reg]" }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\section{Tree Parsing with Tree Automata}\label{sec:tree-automata}

\subsection{Bottom-Up Tree Parsing}\label{sec:bottom-up-parsing}

Instruction selection can be formulated as \emph{bottom-up tree parsing}: starting from the leaves, each subtree is matched against machine patterns, and the best (minimum-cost) match is selected.

\begin{definition}[Bottom-Up Tree Parsing]\label{def:bottom-up-parse}
\textbf{Bottom-up tree parsing} assigns to each node $n$ in the IR tree a set of \emph{nonterminals} that $n$ can produce. For a leaf node, the nonterminals are determined by the leaf patterns. For an internal node, the nonterminals are determined by patterns whose children match the nonterminals of the child nodes.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Tree Automata}\label{sec:tree-automata-theory}

\begin{definition}[Top-Down Tree Automaton]\label{def:top-down-ta}
A \textbf{top-down tree automaton} is a tuple $(Q, \Sigma, \Delta, q_0)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $Q$ is a finite set of states (nonterminals).
  \item $\Sigma$ is a ranked alphabet (IR operators).
  \item $\Delta$ is a set of transitions: $q \to f(q_1, \ldots, q_k)$ for each $f \in \Sigma$ of arity $k$.
  \item $q_0$ is the initial state (the goal: produce a \texttt{reg}).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\section{BURS (Bottom-Up Rewrite System)}\label{sec:burs}

\emph{BURS} is a system for generating optimal instruction selectors from a machine description. It was developed by Peleg, Ipsen, and Patil~\cite{peleg1993burg} and is based on tree grammar reduction.

\subsection{Tree Grammar Formulation}\label{sec:burs-grammar}

\begin{definition}[BURS Tree Grammar]\label{def:burs-grammar}
A \textbf{BURS tree grammar} is a set of productions of the form:
\[
\mathit{nt} \to \mathit{pattern} \quad \text{cost } c
\]
where $\mathit{nt}$ is a nonterminal, $\mathit{pattern}$ is a tree over terminals (IR operators) and nonterminals, and $c$ is the cost.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[BURS Grammar for x86]\label{ex:burs-x86}
\begin{verbatim}
  reg -> CONST(c)           cost 1
  reg -> ADD(reg, reg)      cost 1
  reg -> ADD(reg, CONST(c)) cost 2
  reg -> MUL(reg, reg)      cost 3
  reg -> MEM(reg)           cost 1
  reg -> LOAD(reg)          cost 2
  reg -> reg                 cost 0    (identity: copy)
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Optimal Parsing}\label{sec:burs-optimal}

\begin{definition}[Optimal BURS Parsing]\label{def:burs-optimal}
The \textbf{optimal BURS parsing} of a tree $T$ is the derivation of minimum total cost that produces the start symbol from $T$. This is computed by dynamic programming bottom-up.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{BURS Optimal Parsing}
\label{alg:burs}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require IR tree $T$, BURS grammar $G$
\Ensure Minimum-cost instruction sequence
\ForAll{leaves $l$ of $T$ (bottom-up order)}
  \ForAll{rules $r$ matching $l$}
    \State $\mathit{cost}[l][\mathit{nt}(r)] = \min(\mathit{cost}[l][\mathit{nt}(r)], \mathit{cost}(r))$
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\ForAll{internal nodes $n$ of $T$ (bottom-up)}
  \ForAll{rules $r$ matching $n$}
    \State $c = \mathit{cost}(r) + \sum_{i} \mathit{cost}[\mathit{child}_i(n)][\mathit{nt}_i(r)]$
    \State $\mathit{cost}[n][\mathit{nt}(r)] = \min(\mathit{cost}[n][\mathit{nt}(r)], c)$
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\State Reconstruct optimal derivation from root
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[BURS Optimality]\label{thm:burs-optimal}
The BURS algorithm computes the minimum-cost instruction sequence for any IR tree, provided the grammar is non-ambiguous (or the minimum over all derivations is taken).
\end{theorem}

\section{DAG Covering for Instruction Selection}\label{sec:dag-covering}

When the IR is a DAG (with shared subexpressions), the problem becomes \emph{DAG covering}: find a minimum-cost cover of the DAG using instruction patterns.

\begin{definition}[DAG Covering]\label{def:dag-cover}
A \textbf{DAG cover} is a mapping from each node in the DAG to a set of nonterminals it can produce, such that the total cost is minimized. Unlike tree covering, a node in a DAG may have multiple parents, and its value may be computed once and used multiple times.
\end{definition}

DAG covering is NP-hard in general, but practical algorithms use heuristics or restrict the problem to make it tractable.

\section{Dynamic Programming Approaches}\label{sec:dp-approaches}

\subsection{The IBURG Algorithm}\label{sec:iburg}

\emph{IBURG} is an improvement over BURS that handles the case where patterns have multiple possible costs depending on the context.

\begin{definition}[IBURG]\label{def:iburg}
\textbf{IBURG} extends BURS by allowing \emph{parameterized costs}: the cost of a rule can depend on the nonterminals chosen for its children. This enables more accurate cost modeling.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The TWIG System}\label{sec:twig}

\emph{TWIG}~\cite{chase1986improving} uses dynamic programming on trees with \emph{regular tree grammar} techniques. It handles the case where the grammar is not strictly bottom-up by using a two-pass approach.

\section{Modern Approaches: TableGen, ISel Frameworks}\label{sec:modern-isel}

\subsection{LLVM's TableGen}\label{sec:tablegen}

LLVM uses a \emph{TableGen}-based approach to instruction selection. Machine descriptions are written in a declarative format (.td files), and TableGen generates C++ code for the instruction selector.

\begin{definition}[LLVM SelectionDAG]\label{def:selectiondag}
LLVM's instruction selector operates on a \textbf{SelectionDAG}: a DAG representation of a basic block where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Nodes represent operations and values.
  \item Edges represent data and control dependencies.
  \item Legalization transforms the DAG to use only legal types and operations.
  \item Instruction selection maps DAG nodes to machine instructions.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{ISel Pattern Matching}\label{sec:isel-pattern}

Modern instruction selectors use \emph{predicate-based pattern matching}:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The DAG is traversed bottom-up.
  \item At each node, a set of patterns is tested in priority order.
  \item The first matching pattern determines the instruction emitted.
  \item Patterns include predicates on node types, operand types, and constant values.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Global Instruction Selection}\label{sec:global-isel}

\emph{Global instruction selection} (GlobalISel) in LLVM extends the SelectionDAG approach to handle instructions that span multiple basic blocks, such as tail calls and exception handling.

\begin{historical}
Instruction selection has evolved from hand-coded peephole optimizers to sophisticated automated systems. The BURS approach of Peleg et al.~\cite{peleg1993burg} (1993) was a major advance, providing optimal instruction selection for tree-structured IR. The TWIG system of Chase~\cite{chase1986improving} (1986) extended this to handle more complex grammars. Modern compilers like LLVM and GCC use table-driven approaches that generate instruction selectors from machine descriptions. The trend is toward more declarative, maintainable instruction selection specifications that can be automatically optimized.
\end{historical}

\section{Instruction Selection with Addressing Modes}\label{sec:addressing-modes}

Real instruction selection must handle complex addressing modes, where a single instruction can perform address computation and memory access simultaneously.

\begin{definition}[Addressing Mode Pattern]\label{def:addr-mode}
An \textbf{addressing mode pattern} describes how a memory access can be folded into an instruction. For example, x86's \texttt{mov eax, [ebx + ecx*4 + 8]} performs the computation $ebx + ecx \times 4 + 8$ and loads from the resulting address in a single instruction.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Addressing Mode Matching]\label{ex:addr-mode}
The expression \texttt{load(a + i * 4 + 8)} can be matched by:
\begin{verbatim}
  reg -> LOAD(ADD(ADD(base, MUL(idx, CONST(4))), CONST(8)))
    cost 1; emit "mov reg, [base + idx*4 + 8]"
\end{verbatim}
This avoids separate instructions for the address computation and the load.
\end{example}

The dashed boundary in \Cref{fig:visual-77} marks one instruction
pattern, not an independently emitted instruction at each IR node.

