\section{Legalization}\label{sec:legalization}

\emph{Legalization} transforms the IR to use only operations and types that are supported by the target machine.

\begin{definition}[Legalization]\label{def:legalization}
\textbf{Legalization} transforms the IR to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Replace unsupported types with supported types (e.g., \texttt{i64} on a 32-bit machine becomes two \texttt{i32} operations).
  \item Replace unsupported operations with sequences of supported operations.
  \item Expand complex operations into simpler ones (e.g., \texttt{div} becomes a call to a library function).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Type Legalization}
\label{alg:legalization}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require IR with arbitrary types, target type set $T$
\Ensure IR using only types in $T$
\ForAll{values $v$ with type $\tau \notin T$}
  \State Find smallest type $\tau' \in T$ such that $\tau' \geq \tau$
  \If{$\tau'$ is a vector type}
    \State Split $v$ into vector elements
  \ElsIf{$\tau'$ is wider than $\tau$}
    \State Promote $v$ to $\tau'$ (zero-extend or sign-extend)
  \Else
    \State Expand $v$ into multiple values of smaller types
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Legalizing an Unsigned Comparison into Limbs}

Splitting a wide value into legal types does not by itself say how to split operations on that value. In particular, a comparison must still return a single Boolean; comparing the two low words alone loses the most significant bits, while combining both less-than results with a simple disjunction lets the low word override the high word.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} Compare defined unsigned $2w$-bit values whose low and high $w$-bit limbs are available as SSA values on the target. Limb numbering is by significance, not memory layout. Legal instructions include word equality, word unsigned comparison, and Boolean operations with an agreed representation. This expansion does not apply directly to floating-point or signed comparisons.

\begin{sudocode}[Expand an unsigned wide less-than]
\Require Unsigned wide operands $x,y$, legal word size $w$
\Ensure Legal Boolean equal to the original comparison
\Let{x_h,x_l}{\Call{Limbs}{x}}
\Let{y_h,y_l}{\Call{Limbs}{y}}
\Let{d}{\Call{WordULT}{x_h,y_h}}
\Let{e}{\Call{WordEQ}{x_h,y_h}}
\Let{l}{\Call{WordULT}{x_l,y_l}}
\State $t\gets e\land l$ \Comment{Low words only break a tie}
\State $b\gets d\lor t$ \Comment{A smaller high word suffices}
\State Replace the original Boolean with $b$
\State Queue the new operations for selection
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} With 8-bit limbs, $x=\texttt{0x02FF}$ and $y=\texttt{0x0300}$ give $d=1$, $e=0$, $l=0$, hence $b=1$. For $x=\texttt{0x0201}$ and $y=\texttt{0x0203}$, the high words tie, so $d=0$, $e=1$, $l=1$, and the result is again true. For $x=\texttt{0x0300}$ and $y=\texttt{0x02FF}$, $l=1$ cannot overturn the greater high word: $b=0$.

\textbf{Invariant.} A high-word difference, scaled by $2^w$, exceeds any difference between the low words. The expansion consequently uses a constant number of word operations, even when the selector later combines tests into flags or branches. A signed variant needs a signed high-word comparison, but its tie-break is still unsigned. If the target only supports comparisons that set flags, lower the Boolean combination using explicit flag uses or conditional branches; a later comparison must not silently overwrite an earlier result.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:instruction-selection-summary}

Instruction selection maps the IR to target machine instructions. It is fundamentally a pattern matching problem: covering an IR tree or DAG with minimum-cost machine instruction patterns. Tree automata and BURS provide optimal solutions for tree-structured IR. DAG covering handles shared subexpressions but is NP-hard in general. Addressing modes allow folding address computation into load/store instructions. Legalization ensures the IR uses only types and operations supported by the target. Modern compilers use table-driven approaches (LLVM TableGen) that generate instruction selectors from declarative machine descriptions. The quality of instruction selection directly affects the performance of generated code.

\section{BURS: Bottom-Up Rewrite Systems}\label{sec:burs-detail}

\emph{BURS} (Bottom-Up Rewrite System) is a systematic technique for optimal instruction selection on tree-structured IR.

\begin{definition}[BURS]\label{def:burs}
A \textbf{BURS} is a tree grammar $G = (N, \Sigma, P, S)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $N$ is a set of nonterminals (representing machine resources: registers, conditions)
  \item $\Sigma$ is the set of IR operators
  \item $P$ is a set of productions (tree patterns with costs)
  \item $S$ is the start nonterminal
\end{itemize}
Each production $A \to pattern$ has an associated cost (e.g., execution time or code size).
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[BURS Grammar for x86]\label{ex:burs-grammar}
\begin{verbatim}
  // Nonterminals: reg (register), mem (memory operand)
  // Terminals: CONST, ADD, MUL, LOAD, STORE, reg
  
  // Productions:
  reg → CONST(c)              cost: 1  (mov reg, imm)
  reg → ADD(reg, reg)         cost: 1  (add reg, reg)
  reg → ADD(reg, CONST(c))    cost: 1  (add reg, imm)
  reg → MUL(reg, reg)         cost: 3  (imul reg, reg)
  reg → MUL(reg, CONST(c))    cost: 3  (imul reg, imm)
  reg → LOAD(mem)             cost: 2  (mov reg, [mem])
  mem → reg                   cost: 0  (register addressing)
  mem → ADD(reg, CONST(c))    cost: 0  (base+disp addressing)
  mem → ADD(reg, reg)         cost: 0  (base+index addressing)
  reg → LOAD(ADD(reg, reg))   cost: 2  (mov reg, [base+index])
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{BURS Labeling Algorithm}\label{sec:burs-labeling}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{BURS Tree Labeling (Dynamic Programming)}
\label{alg:burs-labeling}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require IR tree $T$, BURS grammar $G$
\ensure Minimum-cost labeling of $T$
\State Process $T$ bottom-up (leaves to root)
\ForAll{nodes $n$ in bottom-up order}
  \ForAll{productions $p: A \to pattern$ matching at $n$}
    \State Compute cost: $c(p) = \text{production cost} + \sum_{\text{children } c} \text{best\_cost}(c, \text{NT}_p(c))$
    \State Record: $\text{cost}(n, A) = \min(\text{cost}(n, A), c(p))$
    \State Record: $\text{rule}(n, A) = p$ \Comment{Best rule for nonterminal $A$ at $n$}
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\State \Return $\text{cost}(\text{root}, S)$ and trace back rules
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[BURS Optimality]\label{thm:burs-optimal}
BURS finds the minimum-cost instruction selection for tree-structured IR in $O(|T| \cdot |P|)$ time, where $|T|$ is the tree size and $|P|$ is the number of productions. The result is provably optimal among all instruction selections that can be expressed by the grammar.
\end{theorem}

\section{Dynamic Programming for DAG Covering}\label{sec:dp-dag-covering}

When the IR is a DAG (with shared subexpressions), the problem becomes harder because sharing introduces interactions between pattern choices.

\begin{definition}[DAG Covering Problem]\label{def:dag-covering}
The \textbf{DAG covering problem} is: given a DAG $D$ and a set of tree patterns with costs, find a minimum-cost cover of $D$ using the patterns. A cover assigns each node to at least one pattern, and shared nodes must be consistently covered.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[DAG Covering Complexity]\label{thm:dag-covering-complexity}
The DAG covering problem is NP-hard in general. However, for trees (DAGs without sharing), it is solvable in polynomial time by BURS.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Heuristic DAG Covering}\label{sec:heuristic-dag}

\begin{definition}[Maximal Munch]\label{def:maximal-munch}
\textbf{Maximal munch} is a greedy heuristic for DAG covering: at each node, select the largest (most specific) pattern that matches, regardless of whether a smaller pattern might lead to a better global solution.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Maximal Munch Instruction Selection}
\label{alg:maximal-munch}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require IR DAG $D$, pattern set $P$
\ensure Instruction sequence covering $D$
\State Start at root of $D$
\While{uncovered nodes remain}
  \State Find the largest pattern $p \in P$ that matches at current node
  \State Emit instruction for $p$
  \State Mark nodes covered by $p$
  \State Move to next uncovered node (deepest first)
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{example}[Maximal Munch vs.\ Optimal]\label{ex:munch-vs-optimal}
\begin{verbatim}
  // IR tree: ADD(MUL(x, 3), y)
  
  // Maximal munch: matches ADD(MUL(x, 3), y)
  // → lea rax, [rdi + rdi*2]  ; x * 3
  // → add rax, rsi             ; + y
  // Cost: 2 instructions
  
  // But optimal might be:
  // → mov rax, rdi
  // → lea rax, [rax + rax*2 + rsi]  ; x*3 + y in one LEA
  // Cost: 2 instructions (same), but LEA has higher latency
  
  // Maximal munch may miss opportunities where a smaller
  // local match enables a better global solution.
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\section{Table-Driven Instruction Selection}\label{sec:table-driven-isel}

\subsection{LLVM TableGen}\label{sec:tablegen-detail}

LLVM uses \emph{TableGen} to generate instruction selectors from declarative machine descriptions:

\begin{definition}[TableGen Instruction Description]\label{def:tablegen-desc}
A \textbf{TableGen instruction description} specifies:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Instruction format (encoding, operands)
  \item Pattern (SDNode DAG that this instruction implements)
  \item Predicates (when this instruction is available)
  \item Scheduling information (latency, resource usage)
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Example TableGen instruction definition:
  def ADD32rr : Instruction<
    (outs GR32:$dst),
    (ins GR32:$src1, GR32:$src2),
    "add\t{$dst, $src1, $src2}",
    [(set GR32:$dst, (add GR32:$src1, GR32:$src2))]
  > {
    let Constraints = "$src1 = $dst";
    let AsmString = "add\t{$dst, $src2}";
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{SelectionDAG ISel}\label{sec:selectiondag-isel}

LLVM's \emph{SelectionDAG} instruction selector converts the SelectionDAG to machine instructions using a combination of BURS (for simple patterns) and custom C++ code (for complex patterns):

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{SelectionDAG Instruction Selection}
\label{alg:selectiondag}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require SelectionDAG $G$, machine description $M$
\ensure Machine instruction DAG
\ForAll{nodes $n$ in $G$ in topological order}
  \State Try to match $n$ against TableGen patterns
  \If{pattern matched}
    \State Emit machine instruction for matched pattern
  \Else
    \State Call custom selector (C++ code) for $n$
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State Perform post-selection legalization
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Instruction Selection for Complex Addressing Modes}\label{sec:complex-addressing}

\begin{definition}[Address Mode Selection]\label{def:addr-mode-selection}
\textbf{Address mode selection} chooses the most efficient addressing mode for each memory access. Modern x86-64 supports:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{[base]}: base register only
  \item \texttt{[base + disp]}: base + displacement
  \item \texttt{[base + index*scale]}: base + scaled index
  \item \texttt{[base + index*scale + disp]}: full SIB addressing
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Address Mode Selection}
\label{alg:addr-mode}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Address computation DAG for a load/store
\ensure Optimal addressing mode
\State Analyze address computation tree
\If{tree matches \texttt{base + index*scale + disp}}
  \State Use full SIB addressing mode (cost: 0 extra instructions)
\ElsIf{tree matches \texttt{base + disp}}
  \State Use base+disp addressing mode
\ElsIf{tree is a single register}
  \State Use register-indirect addressing
\Else
  \State Compute address into a register using LEA
  \State Use register-indirect addressing (cost: 1 LEA instruction)
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Instruction Selection Quality Metrics}\label{sec:isel-quality}

\begin{definition}[Instruction Selection Quality]\label{def:isel-quality}
The quality of instruction selection is measured by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Code size}: Total bytes of generated code.
  \item \textbf{Execution time}: Estimated cycle count based on instruction latencies.
  \item \textbf{Register pressure}: Number of registers required by the selected instructions.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[BURS vs.\ Heuristic Quality]\label{thm:burs-quality}
BURS produces optimal instruction selection for tree-structured IR. For DAG-structured IR, the gap between maximal munch and optimal is typically 1--5\% in code quality, but maximal munch is $O(n)$ vs.\ $O(n^2)$ or worse for optimal DAG covering.
\end{theorem}
